\section{Focus of the Workshop}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

The rapid adoption of data-centric AI has amplified the demand for large-scale, diverse, and high-quality datasets. However, real-world data is often scarce, sensitive, or biased, creating significant bottlenecks for training and evaluating robust AI systems. Advances in synthetic data generation—powered by Large Language Models (LLMs) and generative AI—are unlocking new possibilities to create realistic, domain-relevant, and privacy-preserving datasets.

The \textbf{SynData@SIGMOD 2026} workshop aims to bring together researchers and practitioners from data management, AI systems, and machine learning to explore the next generation of synthetic data pipelines. The workshop will serve as a leading venue for presenting new research, exchanging ideas, and fostering collaborations at the intersection of databases and AI-driven data generation.

Key themes include:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Architectures and systems for scalable synthetic data generation.
    \item Synergy between data management and LLM-based data synthesis.
    \item Evaluation and benchmarking of fidelity, realism, and downstream utility.
    \item Responsible data generation, including privacy, fairness, and bias mitigation.
    \item Applications of synthetic data generation in data-scarce domains such as enterprise analytics, healthcare, finance, and social systems.
\end{itemize}

These topics are of great relevance for both the SIGMOD and AI communities. While several AI venues (e.g., NeurIPS, ICML, AAAI) explore generative modeling, there is a notable gap in database-focused discussions around the storage, management, validation, and benchmarking of synthetic data. SynData@SIGMOD 2026 bridges this gap by connecting data management researchers with generative AI practitioners to define a coherent research agenda for synthetic data systems.

The workshop’s theme for this edition is: \textit{“Building Trustworthy Synthetic Data Pipelines for Data-Centric AI.”}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Intended Audience and Size}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

The workshop targets researchers, practitioners, and students working in:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Data management and database systems.
    \item Machine learning and generative AI.
    \item Responsible and privacy-preserving data analytics.
    \item Data-centric AI, benchmarking, and data evaluation frameworks.
\end{itemize}

We anticipate 80–120 participants, with representation from both academia and industry, including leading research labs and AI system developers. The workshop is expected to attract attendees from organizations such as Adobe Research, IBM Research, Google DeepMind, Microsoft Research, and academic groups focused on data systems and machine learning.

